{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Data, Information and Knowledge", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 12, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use when talking about data, information and knowledge.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Understand information about data and its structure in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.  Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob12re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Simple Past vs. Simple Present ", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We can use the <span class=\"strong\">simple past</span> tense and the  <span class=\"strong\">simple present </span>tense to describe actions we finished in the past or actions we routinely do.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "We use the simple past tense when we want to describe an activity that we started and finished at some point in the past e.g. last Monday, yesterday, in 2010, etc. In other words, there is not a direct connection to the present time when we are talking.</br></br> Example: <br /><span class=\"italic\">Yesterday in class, we <span class=\"strong\">studied</span> the difference between knowledge, data and information.</span></br></br>  On the other hand, we use the present simple tense when we want to describe general or habitual activities that are repeated regularly e.g. every day, on weekends, every Tuesday, etc. Generally, the simple present tense refers to habits or routines.</br></br>  Example:<br /> <span class=\"italic\">Every semester, I <span class=\"strong\">watch</span> the movie \u201cInside Out\u201d with my students. It <span class=\"strong\">explains</span> in a friendly manner some \t\t\tcomplicated topics like memory and behavior.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "In the past, we had a different concept of information and data, these days, data, information and knowledge are related one to each other.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "In the past, humans could only build knowledge of the world by direct experience. Before they started storing data as information, you couldn't know a person's height by looking at a database; you had to measure or ask the person directly. Nowadays, we can stored it in computes and check it whenever we want. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We can use these two tenses in a sentence to show contrast between the past and the present. </br></br>Example: </br><i>In the past, people <b>didn't have</b> to worry about moral standards in technology, nowadays, people <b>exceed</b> the limits of life using it.</i>", "type": "text"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Modal verbs can, could and should (review)", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We use the modal verbs <span class=\"strong\"> can, could and should</span> for expressing abilities and recommendations. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": " We use can and could to describe skills or possibilities. In the present tense we use can, but in the past tense we use could. Also, could can be used for requests and suggestions. </br></br>   Examples:</br><br /> <span class=\"strong\">Skills</span></br> <span class=\"italic\">They <b>can</b> store data, but they <b>can't</b> store knowledge. </br>She <b>could</b> store data, but she <b>couldn't</b> store knowledge.</br><br /><span class=\"strong\">Possibilities</span></br><i> We <b>could</b> understand better the difference if we had payed attention.</span></br><b><br />Requests</b></br><i><b>Could/Can</b> you explain me the difference between knowledge, data and information?</i></br></br>  On the other hand, we use should to give advice. It expresses a mild obligation or moral responsibility. </br></br>  Examples: </br> <span class=\"italic\">People <b>should</b> store their data in safer ways.</br>You <b>shouldn't</b> share your data with everyone. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You can only keep knowledge in a human brain.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We should be careful when defining each one of them.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "In the past humans could only build knowledge of the world by direct experience.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "simply put ", "type": "name"}, {"content": " in clear terms", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Simply put, knowledge is what we know and is stored in our brains. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob12aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "independent  ", "type": "name"}, {"content": " without any intervention or help", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Computers can't make independent decisions.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob12aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "height ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "body extent measured from lowest to highest point ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Data are the facts in reality, for example your age or your height.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob12aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "sense   ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "any of the human faculties to perceive reality", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Data is a description of the real world as we perceive it with our senses.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob12aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "beyond ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "outside the limits of something  ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(preposition)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Information is how humans expand their knowledge beyond the data collected by their senses.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob12aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "looks    ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "aspect or appearance", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You can send, transform or even destroy the photograph, but your looks in reality are not affected.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob12aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Look for more evidence about the difference between data, information and knowledge.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write the important differences in a comparative chart.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share the comparative chart with your partners.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I look for evidence related with data, information and knowledge.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write about data, information and knowledge in a comparative chart.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share information with my partners.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "